            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"The path of holiness, salvation, and right relationship with God; a prepared spiritual route for God's presence or the believer's journey of sanctification.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Isaiah 35:8\",\n    \"Isaiah 40:3\",\n    \"Proverbs 16:17\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Repentance and the removal of spiritual obstacles in the human heart to prepare for the Lord's coming.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Matthew 3:3\",\n        \"Mark 1:3\",\n        \"Luke 3:4\",\n        \"Isaiah 40:3\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Eschatological restoration, peace, and the ingathering of exiles and Gentile nations to Zion.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Isaiah 11:16\",\n        \"Isaiah 19:23\",\n        \"Isaiah 62:10\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"An inner posture of pilgrimage, devotion, and longing for God's presence.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Psalm 84:5\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"The 'highway' (often translating the Hebrew 'mesillah') in prophetic literature frequently symbolizes God making an accessible, holy way through the wilderness of sin or exile. In the New Testament, it is most famously applied to the ministry of John the Baptist, representing the preparation of the people's hearts for the arrival of the Messiah.\"\n}",
